-- title:
--   Generators of the blow-up chart C[J/a] are integral modulo y+(varpiₜ/a)
-- statement:
--   Setting. Let $q\ge 5$ and $\ell\ge 3$ be primes with $\ell\neq q$, and let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number divisible by neither $q$ nor $\ell$ (`hq`, `hℓ3`, `hℓq`, `hqM'`, `hℓM'`). Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero carrying a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity $\xi$ (`hξ`) for which some ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb{C}$ sends $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$ (`hι`). Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ which, by `hK`, is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79): that is, $K$ is generated over $L$ by the coefficientwise images of the $\mathbb{Q}$-rational function field of the modular curve of level $N_0=(q\ell)^2M'$ attached to the subgroup $H=\ker\bigl((\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times\bigr)$ of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$.
--
--   Let $A$ be a henselian discrete valuation domain with algebraically closed residue field, an $L$-algebra with $L$ as its fraction field, such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hξA`); let $K$ be an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j\in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function (`hj`). Fix a uniformiser $\varpi$ of $A$ (`hϖ`) and a tame constant $\varpi_t\in A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=qu$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j`, the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin A ↥K j` for $j$ viewed in $C$. Let $y\subseteq C$ be a maximal ideal (`hy`) containing $\varpi$ (`hϖy`) which is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nontrivial $q$-torsion point.
--
--   The Drinfeld chart hypothesis `hArig`. This hypothesis asserts, for every point $z$ of the two-chart integral model $X=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) (the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the two chart algebras over the middle chart) at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ pulled back from $\operatorname{Spec} A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hϖz`, `hz`), and for every point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` (`hy'`) and supersingular in the above sense (`hss'`), the existence of a complete discrete valuation domain $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma:A\to W$ with $\sigma\varpi$ a uniformiser, power series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f\equiv X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk of $X$ at $z$ onto $S=W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))v-fu\bigr)$, subject to seven conditions formulated in terms of the completion map $\mathrm{toC}$, the quotient map $\mathrm{mkS}$ and the canonical map $\mathrm{germY}:C\to\mathcal{O}_{X,z}$: (1) $e$ carries the germ of $a\in A$ to $C(\sigma a)$; (2) every automorphism $\tau$ of $K/L$ which is a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$, in the sense of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) (the condition comparing the $\iota$-image of $\tau x$ with the $\gamma$-translated $q$-expansions of the modular forms whose quotient represents $x$), maps $C$ into $C$; (3) if moreover $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, such a $\tau$ acts trivially on $C$ modulo $y'$; (4) for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and such a $\tau$ acting trivially modulo $y'$, there are an automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ compatible with $\tau$ through $e\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$, a scalar $c\in W$ and a matrix $M$ over $W$ such that $\theta$ fixes the constants, $\theta(X_j)\equiv\sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the images of $X_0,X_1$, $c^{q+1}\equiv 1$, $M_{ij}\equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo the maximal ideal of $W$, $c\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, and $c\not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity; (5) two primes of $S$ avoiding one of the images of $X_0,X_1$, containing the image of $C(\sigma\varpi)$ and containing linear forms $a_iX_0+b_iX_1$ up to $(X_0,X_1)^2$ with $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ have distinct contractions along $e\circ\mathrm{toC}$; (6) for a prime $P$ of the same kind containing $X_0$ up to $(X_0,X_1)^2$ and for $a\in C$, the germ of $a$ lies in the contraction of $P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of the maximal ideal of $A$; (7) the Laurent series [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, and for a suitable $a_0\in A$ with the difference lying in $y'$, there are $e_0\ge 1$ and $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form $\sum_{i\le e_0}\mathrm{coeff}_{(i,e_0-i)}(h)\,a^ib^{e_0-i}$ is a unit for all $a,b\in W$ not both in the maximal ideal with $a^qb-ab^q$ in the maximal ideal, such that $e$ carries the germ of that difference to the class of $h$.
--
--   The linked witness. Fixed data $z,\varpi_z,y'$ as above with `hϖz`, `hz`, `hy'`, `hss'`, together with `hy'y` identifying $y'$ with $y$, and a specific witness $W_1$, $\sigma_1$, $f_1,u_1,v_1$, $e_1$ of the shape just described (`hσ₁`, `hu₁`, `hv₁`, `hf₁`); the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this witness exactly the seven conditions (1)–(7) listed above, with $W_1,\sigma_1,e_1$ in place of $W,\sigma,e$.
--
--   The centre and the rings $B$, $W$. By `hJ`, the ideal $J\subseteq C$ is the infimum of the ideals obtained as follows: for $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, take the contraction along $e_1\circ\mathrm{toC}\circ\mathrm{germY}$ of the ideal of $S$ generated by the classes of $C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, and then its contraction along the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$. By `hB`, $B$ is the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ underlying the $C$-subalgebra generated by $\{x\in K:\exists\,i\in J,\ x\varpi_t=i\}$, that is $C[J/\varpi_t]$. Further, $W$ is a valuation subring of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`), and four groups of hypotheses are imposed: `hR1`, that $C\subseteq B$ and every element of $K$ is a quotient of elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2`, that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and that $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses), that $W$ meets $L$ exactly in the image of $A$, that the maximal ideal of $W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that $y$ is the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W$ to $C$, and that $W$ consists of the quotients $g/h$ with $g,h\in B$ and $h$ a unit of $W$; and `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here), that for each $(\mathrm{GF}(q^2))$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) over that residue field, with kernel the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W$, compatible with $A\to\kappa_A$ and equivariant for the level automorphisms through an element $(\mathrm{redQ}_q\gamma,c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c\neq 1$ whenever $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; that level automorphisms for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B$; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is reached by translating the centre of $W$ under some $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and corresponding $\tau$; that an element of $B$ all of whose level translates lie in the maximal ideal of $W$ is divisible by $\varpi$; and that a level automorphism preserving $y$ on $C$ preserves $W$.
--
--   Conclusion. Let $a\in C$ with $a\in J$ and $a\neq 0$ as an element of $K$, and let $B_a$ be the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ underlying the $C$-subalgebra generated by $\{x\in K:\exists\,i\in J,\ xa=i\}$, that is the affine blow-up chart $C[J/a]$. Then for every $i\in J$ there exist a monic polynomial $p\in A[X]$, an element $b'\in B_a$, a natural number $n$ and families $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to C$ and $b:\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to K$ such that $c_k\in y$ for every $k$, $b_k\in B_a$ for every $k$, and, in $K$,
--   $$p\bigl(i\,a^{-1}\bigr)=\varpi_t\,a^{-1}\,b'+\sum_{k}c_k\,b_k,$$
--   the polynomial being evaluated at $i a^{-1}$ through the structure map $A\to K$. Thus each generator $i/a$ of $B_a$ over $C$ satisfies a monic equation with coefficients in $A$ modulo the ideal $yB_a+(\varpi_t/a)B_a$.
--
--   This is the integrality step in the study of the exceptional fibre of the weighted blow-up of the Drinfeld chart at a supersingular point, in the sense of the exceptional curve of Katz–Mazur: on the locus cut out by $y$ and $\varpi_t/a$ the coordinates $i/a$ of the blow-up chart become integral over the base. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.isMaximal_of_isPrime_of_supersingular_le_of_div_mem_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.isMaximal_of_isPrime_of_supersingular_le_of_div_mem_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked), where it yields that a prime of $B_a$ containing $yB_a+(\varpi_t/a)B_a$ is maximal.
